\f0\fs36 \cf0 \
\
String Similarity \'96 Self-Healing in Test Automation\
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=We3YDTzNXEk\
\
Concept:\
String similarity means measuring how close two strings are.\
\
Example:\
"login_btn" vs "login_button"\
These are similar but not exactly the same.\
\
--------------------------------------------------\
Main Idea (Edit Distance)\
--------------------------------------------------\
Edit distance = number of changes needed to make two strings equal.\
\
Types of changes:\
1. Insert (add character)\
2. Delete (remove character)\
3. Replace (change character)\
\
Example:\
"login_btn" 
\f1 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  "login_button"\
Distance = 5 (5 insert operations)\
\
Rule:\
Lower distance = more similar\
Higher distance = less similar\
\
--------------------------------------------------\
Why this is important in QA\
--------------------------------------------------\
Problem:\
Locator changes break automation tests.\
\
Old locator:\
id = "login_btn"\
\
New locator:\
id = "login_button"\
\
Test fails 
\f2 \uc0\u10060 
\f0 \
\
--------------------------------------------------\
Solution (Self-Healing)\
--------------------------------------------------\
System:\
1. Takes old locator\
2. Compares with current elements\
3. Finds closest match\
4. Uses it automatically\
\
Result:\
Test continues without failure 
\f2 \uc0\u9989 
\f0 \
\
--------------------------------------------------\
How Self-Healing Works\
--------------------------------------------------\
Step 1: Get old locator\
Step 2: Collect new UI locators\
Step 3: Calculate similarity\
Step 4: Choose best match\
\
--------------------------------------------------\
Python Implementation (Edit Distance)\
--------------------------------------------------\
\
def edit_distance(s1, s2):\
    dp = [[0]*(len(s2)+1) for _ in range(len(s1)+1)]\
\
    for i in range(len(s1)+1):\
        dp[i][0] = i\
    for j in range(len(s2)+1):\
        dp[0][j] = j\
\
    for i in range(1, len(s1)+1):\
        for j in range(1, len(s2)+1):\
            if s1[i-1] == s2[j-1]:\
                dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]\
            else:\
                dp[i][j] = 1 + min(\
                    dp[i-1][j],      # delete\
                    dp[i][j-1],      # insert\
                    dp[i-1][j-1]     # replace\
                )\
\
    return dp[-1][-1]\
\
--------------------------------------------------\
Find Best Matching Locator\
--------------------------------------------------\
\
old_locator = "login_btn"\
\
available_locators = [\
    "login_button",\
    "signup_btn",\
    "home_link",\
    "login_link"\
]\
\
best_match = None\
lowest_distance = float('inf')\
\
for locator in available_locators:\
    dist = edit_distance(old_locator, locator)\
    print(f"\{locator\} -> distance = \{dist\}")\
\
    if dist < lowest_distance:\
        lowest_distance = dist\
        best_match = locator\
\
print("\\nBest Match:", best_match)\
\
--------------------------------------------------\
Expected Output Example\
--------------------------------------------------\
\
login_button -> distance = 5\
signup_btn   -> distance = 4\
home_link    -> distance = 8\
login_link   -> distance = 4\
\
Best Match: login_button\
\
--------------------------------------------------\
Real QA Meaning\
--------------------------------------------------\
- Instead of failing when locator changes 
\f2 \uc0\u10060 
\f0 \
- System auto-selects closest locator 
\f2 \uc0\u9989 
\f0 \
- Improves stability of automation tests\
\
--------------------------------------------------\
Where Used\
--------------------------------------------------\
- Selenium self-healing frameworks\
- AI-based automation tools\
- Dynamic UI locator recovery\
\
